ut careful: `str.isdigit()` returns True for Unicode digits like '²' (superscript two) — `'\\u00b2'.isdigit()` is True! And `int('²')` raises ValueError. Hmm. But wait — can a Unicode digit even appear in a matched identifier? The regex only allows ASCII `[0-9a-zA-Z-]`, so identifiers from the regex are ASCII-only. So `isdigit()` is safe on regex-validated identifiers. But to be extra clean, use `.isdecimal()`? For ASCII digits both are equivalent. Since identifiers come from the regex, only ASCII chars present, so `isdigit()` ↔ all chars in 0-9. Safe. But I'll use the fullyear approach... no, keep it simple: `ident.isdigit()`.\n\nHmm, but let me double check the alphanumeric classification: identifier is numeric iff all chars are digits. Per spec: \"numeric identifiers\" are identifiers comprising only digits. So `ident.isdigit()` on an ASCII-only string exactly tests \"comprises only [0-9]\". \n\nNow the key function:\n\n```python\ndef _precedence_key(version):\n    m = _SEMVER_RE.match(version)\n    if m is None:\n        raise ValueError(f\"invalid semantic version: {version!r}\")\n    major, minor, patch, prerelease, _build = m.groups()\n    core = (int(major), int(minor), int(patch if patch is not None else 0))\n    ...\n```\n\nWait — group numbering: with the optional patch group `(?:\\.(0|[1-9]\\d*))?`, group 3 is patch, may be None → 0.\n\nPrerelease key:\n\n```python\nif prerelease is None:\n    pre_key = (1, ())\nelse:\n    pre_key = (0, tuple((0, int(i)) if i.isdigit() else (1, i) for i in prerelease.split('.')))\n```\n\nWait — I need the flag ordering: no-prerelease must sort HIGHER. Using tuple comparison on `(flag, ids)`: version without prerelease gets flag 1, with gets flag 0. Then `(0, ids) < (1, ())` → prerelease < release. Correct. And when both flags equal, compare ids tuples. But careful — comparing `(0, ids1)` vs `(0, ids2)` where ids are tuples of (int, int/str): elementwise fine. And ids tuple comparison: `(0, 5) < (0, 12)` fine; `(0, 5) < (1, 'alpha')` fine (0<1, second never compared). \n\nHmm wait, one potential pitfall: comparing tuples `(0, int)` vs `(0, int)` fine; but what about comparing ids tuples of different lengths where prefix equal — e.g., `(0,5)` vs `(0,5),(1,'x')` → first equal, then left exhausted → shorter is smaller. Correct per spec (fewer fields → lower precedence). \n\nAnd alphanumeric identifiers compared as strings: Python compares by Unicode code point = ASCII for these chars. Correct.\n\nFull key: `core + pre_key`? Or a tuple `(major, minor, patch) + pre_key` → `(major, minor, patch, flag, ids_tuple)`. Yes.\n\nThen:\n\n```python\ndef newest(versions):\n    best = None\n    best_key = None\n    for v in versions:\n        key = _precedence_key(v)\n        if best_key is None or key > best_key:\n            best_key = key\n            best = v\n    return best\n```\n\nThis returns the FIRST occurrence of the maximum (strict `>`), single pass, lazy — but note: invalid version raises only when encountered. For `['1.2.3', 'bad']` → ValueError when reaching 'bad'. Good. For all-invalid → ValueError. Good. Empty → None. Good.\n\nHmm wait, but one subtlety with lazy validation: `newest(['1.2.3'])` never validates... it does — the loop processes every element, validating each. Good. Single pass, O(n). No full list materialization. \n\nAlternatively use `max()` with key — but `max` computes keys for all elements anyway (raising on invalid), returns first maximal. Equivalent. But the explicit loop avoids storing keys. Either fine; I'll use `max` with a key function for clarity:\n\n```python\ndef newest(versions):\n    return max(versions, key=_precedence_key, default=None)\n```\n\n`max(iterable, key=..., default=None)` — handles empty iterables (including generators!) and returns first maximal element. That's concise and correct. Wait — does `max` with key compute key once per element? Yes. And with `default=None`, empty → None. \n\nHmm, but does `max` return the first or last maximal element? Python docs: \"If multiple items are maximal, the function returns the first one encountered.\" Yes, first. Ties → either spelling acceptable. \n\nBut wait, there's one edge: `max` with `key` on an iterable — if `versions` is a dict, iterating gives keys. Whatever, fine.\n\nLet me now double-check the regex more carefully by writing it out and testing mentally, then actually test with Python.\n\nRegex (with re.VERBOSE, whitespace ignored, need to escape literal spaces — none needed):\n\n```python\n_SEMVER_RE = re.compile(\n    r\"\"\"v?\n    (0|[1-9]\\d*)\n    \\.\n    (0|[1-9]\\d*)\n    (?:\\.(0|[1-9]\\d*))?\n    (?:\n        -\n        (\n            (?:0|[1-9]\\d*|\\d*[a-zA-Z-][0-9a-zA-Z-]*)\n            (?:\\.(?:0|[1-9]\\d*|\\d*[a-zA-Z-][0-9a-zA-Z-]*))*\n        )\n    )?\n    (?:\\+([0-9a-zA-Z-]+(?:\\.[0-9a-zA-Z-]+)*))?\n    $\"\"\",\n    re.VERBOSE,\n)\n```\n\nWait — `re.match` vs fullmatch: I use `match` with trailing `$`. `$` matches at end or before trailing newline! So `'1.2.3\\n'` would MATCH with `$`. Hmm. Is `'1.2.3\\n'` valid? Strictly no. Use `\\Z` instead of `$` to be strict. Or `re.fullmatch` without anchors (but with fullmatch, `$` irrelevant; pattern doesn't need `^`/`$`). I'll use `re.fullmatch` semantics: keep the pattern but use `.fullmatch()` and drop `$`/`^`. Cleaner and strict. Note `\\Z` vs fullmatch equivalent. I'll use fullmatch.\n\nCareful with re.VERBOSE and the `-` — fine. Also in VERBOSE mode, `#` starts a comment — my pattern has no `#`. And whitespace in character classes is significant — my classes `[0-9a-zA-Z-]` have no spaces. OK.\n\nOne more check on the prerelease identifier alternative: `\\d*[a-zA-Z-][0-9a-zA-Z-]*` — covers identifiers with at least one letter/hyphen anywhere after optional leading digits. What about identifier `-abc`? `\\d*` = '', `[a-zA-Z-]` = '-', then 'abc' → matches. Good, hyphen-leading identifiers valid per spec.\n\nWhat about identifier that is just `-`? `\\d*`='', `[a-zA-Z-]`='-', then `*` = ''