ne fails. Good — that matches my earlier decision.\n\nPrerelease identifier pattern from official spec: `(?:0|[1-9]\\d*|\\d*[a-zA-Z-][0-9a-zA-Z-]*)` — this covers: pure numeric without leading zeros (`0`, `123`), or anything containing at least one letter or hyphen (which starts with digits optionally then a letter/hyphen). Note `alpha-1` → matches `\\d*[a-zA-Z-]...`? `alpha-1`: starts with `a` (no digits), contains hyphen — yes matches. `01` → all digits with leading zero → none of the alternatives match → invalid. Good. `0a` → `\\d*` = `0`, then `[a-zA-Z-]` = `a` → valid alphanumeric identifier. Correct per spec (leading-zero rule only applies to pure numeric identifiers).\n\nBuild metadata pattern: `[0-9a-zA-Z-]+(?:\\.[0-9a-zA-Z-]+)*` — but strictly, numeric identifiers in build metadata MUST NOT have leading zeros either. The official regex from semver.org is exactly that (doesn't enforce leading zeros in build). Let me check: the official semver.org regex is:\n\n```\n^(0|[1-9]\\d*)\\.(0|[1-9]\\d*)\\.(0|[1-9]\\d*)(?:-((?:0|[1-9]\\d*|\\d*[a-zA-Z-][0-9a-zA-Z-]*)(?:\\.(?:0|[1-9]\\d*|\\d*[a-zA-Z-][0-9a-zA-Z-]*))*))?(?:\\+([0-9a-zA-Z-]+(?:\\.[0-9a-zA-Z-]+)*))?$\n```\n\nYes — build metadata uses `[0-9a-zA-Z-]+` which allows leading zeros in numeric identifiers (e.g., `+001`). So `1.2.3+001` is VALID per the official regex. Interesting — the spec text says numeric build identifiers must not have leading zeros, but the official regex doesn't enforce it. Since the official regex is the reference implementation of the spec, I'll use it as-is: build allows leading zeros. That reduces risk both ways: `1.2.3+001` accepted (per official regex), and precedence unaffected by build anyway.\n\nHmm, wait — actually let me double-check the spec text §10: \"Identifiers MUST comprise only ASCII alphanumerics and hyphen [0-9A-Za-z-]. Identifiers MUST NOT be empty. Numeric identifiers MUST NOT include leading zeroes.\" So per text, `+001` is invalid. Per the official regex, valid. Conflict within the spec itself. I'll follow the official regex (it's the canonical reference). Low-stakes either way.\n\nPrecedence comparison (§11):\n1. Compare major, minor, patch numerically.\n2. If equal and no prerelease → higher than with prerelease.\n3. Both prerelease: compare identifiers left to right:\n   - numeric vs numeric: numeric comparison\n   - alphanumeric vs alphanumeric: ASCII lexical\n   - numeric < alphanumeric\n   - fewer fields < more fields (if all preceding equal)\n\nBuild metadata ignored.\n\nImplementation approach: parse each version string into a sort key, then pick max. Sort key must handle: prerelease identifiers where numeric < alphanumeric — trick: for each identifier, use tuple `(0, int_value, '')` for numeric, `(1, 0, str_value)` for alphanumeric. And the \"fewer fields < more fields\" rule: with tuple comparison, if all preceding equal, then a shorter list is smaller... but we need shorter list = LOWER precedence. In Python tuple/list comparison, `[a] < [a, b]` → True, meaning shorter is smaller. SemVer: \"A larger set of pre-release fields has a higher precedence than a smaller set, if all of the preceding identifiers are equal.\" So `1.0.0-alpha < 1.0.0-alpha.1` → the larger set (alpha.1) is higher. So longer list is greater — that matches Python's natural list comparison. \n\nAnd \"no prerelease > prerelease\": version without prerelease should have higher precedence. So key could be: `(major, minor, patch, no_prerelease_flag, prerelease_ids)`. With no prerelease: `(major, minor, patch, 1, ())`; with: `(major, minor, patch, 0, ids_tuple)`. Comparing `(1,2,3,0,ids)` vs `(1,2,3,1,())`: 0 < 1 → prerelease lower. Good. If both have prerelease: flag equal, compare ids tuples — Python compares elementwise, shorter is smaller when prefix equal. That's exactly the spec rule. \n\nFor numeric identifiers: `(0, num, '')`. For alphanumeric: `(1, 0, text)` — wait, but comparing `(0, num, '')` vs `(1, 0, text)`: first element 0 < 1 → numeric <