ut `'1٢.2.3'`? `\\d*` after `[1-9]` matches '٢' → major = '1٢' → `int('1٢')`?? Python's `int()` DOES accept Unicode decimal digits: `int('1٢')` → 12! Hmm wow. So `'1٢.2.3'` would be accepted as version with major=12?? That's... weird but harmless? It won't raise. Is that a problem? A hidden test with Arabic-Indic digits — extremely unlikely. But for cleanliness I could add `re.ASCII` flag to make `\\d` = [0-9] and also affects `\\w` etc. With re.ASCII, `\\d` matches only [0-9]. Then '1٢' fails ✓. Let me add `re.ASCII` — strictly better. Hmm, does re.ASCII affect anything else in my pattern? `[a-zA-Z0-9]` classes are explicit — unaffected. `\\d` only. Yes, add it.\n\nWait, but with re.ASCII — also `int()` on pure-ASCII digits fine. ✓\n\nAlternatively use `[0-9]` explicitly everywhere instead of `\\d`. With re.ASCII flag, `\\d` is [0-9]. I'll use explicit `[0-9]` AND not rely on the flag... actually let me use `\\d` + re.ASCII — official regex style. Hmm, you know what, let me just use explicit `[0-9]` in character classes to be bulletproof regardless of flags, and ALSO pass re.ASCII. Belt and suspenders — no, keep it readable. I'll use `\\d` with re.ASCII flag. Fine.\n\nHmm, actually one more subtle thing: `ident.isdigit()` — with re.ASCII, identifiers are ASCII-only, so isdigit ⟺ all-ASCII-digits ✓.\n\nAlright, also the alphanumeric key uses the raw identifier string — ASCII by construction ✓.\n\nLet me also handle the `patch` None: `int(patch) if patch else 0` — careful: patch could be '0' which is truthy ('0' is a non-empty string → truthy). `int(patch) if patch is not None else 0`. ✓\n\nNow write the final module:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Semantic-version aware selection of the newest version string.\"\"\"\n\nimport re\n\n# SemVer 2.0.0 grammar (https://semver.org/), relaxed to allow an optional\n# leading \"v\" and an omitted patch component (treated as zero).\n_SEMVER_RE = re.compile(\n    r\"\"\"v?\n    (0|[1-9]\\d*)\\.(0|[1-9]\\d*)(?:\\.(0|[1-9]\\d*))?\n    (?:\n 